<p>However the ocean does not have a constant density stratification. Indeed, the ocean’s density stratification is not only depth-dependent but seasonal as well. As a consequence, the theory developed in <a href="https://doi.org/10.1175/1520-0469(1978)035&lt;0774:UPVFPI&gt;2.0.CO;2">Blumen (1978)</a> and <a href="https://doi.org/10.1017/S0022112095000012">Held et al. (1995)</a> is only relevant at horizontal scales smaller than 10 km in the mid-latitude open ocean. At larger scales, the ocean’s density stratification must be taken into account. In my dissertation, I generalize the theory to account for vertically variable density stratification. The resulting difference between summertime and wintertime turbulence is striking.</p>

<p>In summer, the turbulence is diffuse and highly local in space:</p>
